{
  "dom_id": "step-q85636",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/89426d77a95ffb1a4780484ed1e1ad0531ccd1c4022419ef2c573a08922bf7eb.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[-1,2]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Note: figure not drawn to scale.*",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4-{x}^{2}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+\u2026+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]\u0394x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[-1,2],$ so we have $a=-1$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[f(-1)+2(f(0)+f(1))+f(2)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA\u2248\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+3)+0]\\cdot 1=\\frac{17}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[-1,2],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area.",
